   1: # window-gauge.ps1 - read the Anthropic usage windows and say what may proceed.
   2: #
   3: # WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. Claude Code delivers `rate_limits` to the STATUS LINE and nowhere
   4: # else - hooks receive a different payload entirely - so statusline.ps1 snapshots it on every
   5: # refresh and this script reads that snapshot.
   6: #
   7: # ONE LINE ONLY (founder 2026-08-06): OK below the pause line, PAUSE at it, UNKNOWN when the
   8: # reading cannot be trusted. Two consequences worth holding on to:
   9: #   1. The reading is only as fresh as the last status-line refresh, which happens on every turn
  10: #      of the interactive session. While the coordinator is dormant, nothing updates it - which
  11: #      is exactly why the conductor sends a keep-alive pulse: a pulse wakes the coordinator, the
  12: #      wake refreshes the status line, and the refresh refreshes this gauge. The heartbeat is
  13: #      what keeps the gauge honest, not a timer inside this script.
  14: #   2. A stale reading is NOT a low reading. Age is reported and, past the limit, the verdict is
  15: #      UNKNOWN - never OK. Treating "I cannot see" as "all clear" is how a guard fails silently.
  16: #
  17: # WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It reads and reports. It never pauses anything, never messages an agent,
  18: # never waits. The coordinator owns every decision; this is its instrument, not a second
  19: # authority. A gauge that acts is a brake nobody can see.
  20: #
  21: # The account reports two windows today (five_hour, seven_day). The binary supports three more
  22: # (seven_day_overage_included, seven_day_sonnet, seven_day_opus) which appear on other plans, so
  23: # every window present is evaluated by name rather than assumed - a plan change must not silently
  24: # narrow the guard to the windows we happened to know about.
