FILE loop/work/guard-branch-switch.ps1
   1: # guard-branch-switch.ps1 - refuse a branch change in the MAIN worktree.
   2: #
   3: # WHY THIS EXISTS. A branch belongs to a DIRECTORY, not to a session. Several sessions are
   4: # routinely open in the main folder at once; when any one of them switches branch, every other
   5: # session in that folder is silently moved too, mid-turn, with no notification. On 2026-08-06 two
   6: # sessions shared the main tree - one building a CI runner, one answering questions - and the
   7: # branch was moved under each of them repeatedly. A `git switch main` was refused by git only
   8: # because it happened to notice uncommitted files it would overwrite. That near miss is this file.
   9: #
  10: # The rule already existed in the work skill ("Never check out an item branch, or follow an agent
  11: # into its worktree") and in CLAUDE.md ("a session works where it was launched, on one branch, for
  12: # the whole item"). Both were broken the same day by two different actors, neither careless:
  13: # switching is simply the obvious move when you are in the wrong branch. A rule you must remember
  14: # mid-task is broken during exactly the tasks that matter, so this makes the move impossible
  15: # rather than discouraged.
  16: #
  17: # WHAT IT PROTECTS - the MAIN worktree only, derived rather than hardcoded: git resolves
  18: # --git-dir and --git-common-dir to the same path in the main worktree, and to different paths in
  19: # every linked one. So item worktrees, agent worktrees and the design tree are all untouched, and
  20: # a machine with the repo cloned somewhere else is protected too without configuration.
  21: #
  22: # THE ESCAPE HATCH IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOT A FLAG. Hooks only see tool calls, so a founder
  23: # typing the same command in their own terminal is unaffected. A guard that can be turned off by
  24: # the thing it guards is decoration.
  25: #
  26: # Contract: PreToolUse. Exit 0 allows; exit 2 blocks and sends stderr back to the agent.
  27: # Never throws - a guard that crashes must fail OPEN, because breaking every shell command in
  28: # every session is far worse than the collision it prevents.
  29: 
  30: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  31: 
  32: try {
  33:     $raw = [Console]::In.ReadToEnd()
  34:     if (-not $raw) { exit 0 }
  35:     $j = $null
  36:     try { $j = ConvertFrom-Json $raw } catch { exit 0 }
  37: 
  38:     $cmd = [string]$j.tool_input.command
  39:     if (-not $cmd) { exit 0 }
  40: 
  41:     # Cheap reject first: the overwhelming majority of commands are not git at all.
  42:     if ($cmd -notmatch '(?i)\bgit\b') { exit 0 }
  43:     if ($cmd -notmatch '(?i)\bgit\b[^;|&]*\b(switch|checkout)\b') { exit 0 }
  44: 
  45:     # `git checkout -- <path>` and `git checkout <commit> -- <path>` restore FILES; they do not
  46:     # move the branch. Allowing them matters: refusing a file restore would push people toward
  47:     # deleting and rewriting files by hand, which is worse than what is being prevented.
  48:     if ($cmd -match '(?i)\bgit\b[^;|&]*\b(switch|checkout)\b[^;|&]*\s--\s') { exit 0 }
  49: 
  50:     # `git worktree add` checks a branch out, but into a NEW directory - that is the remedy this
  51:     # guard recommends, so it must never be blocked by it.
  52:     if ($cmd -match '(?i)\bgit\b[^;|&]*\bworktree\b') { exit 0 }
  53: 
  54:     # Which directory would the command act on? `-C <path>` retargets git; otherwise the session's
  55:     # own working directory. Reading the flag matters - a command run from elsewhere with
  56:     # `-C <main tree>` moves the main tree just as effectively.
  57:     $dir = [string]$j.cwd
  58:     $m = [regex]::Match($cmd, '(?i)\bgit\s+(?:-c\s+\S+\s+)*-C\s+"?([^"\s]+)"?')
  59:     if ($m.Success) { $dir = $m.Groups[1].Value }
  60:     if (-not $dir) { exit 0 }
  61:     if (-not (Test-Path $dir)) { exit 0 }
  62: 
  63:     $gitDir    = (& git -C $dir rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>$null)
  64:     $commonDir = (& git -C $dir rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>$null)
  65:     if (-not $gitDir -or -not $commonDir) { exit 0 }   # not a repo, or git unavailable: allow
  66: 
  67:     $gitDir    = $gitDir.Trim().Replace('\','/').TrimEnd('/')
  68:     $commonDir = $commonDir.Trim().Replace('\','/').TrimEnd('/')
  69: 
  70:     # Linked worktree - its own directory, its own branch, nobody else's problem.
  71:     if ($gitDir -ne $commonDir) { exit 0 }
  72: 
  73:     $branch = (& git -C $dir rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>$null)
  74:     if ($branch) { $branch = $branch.Trim() }
  75: 
  76:     $msg = @"
  77: BLOCKED: this is the MAIN worktree, and a branch belongs to the directory, not to your session.
  78: 
  79:   directory : $dir
  80:   branch    : $branch
  81:   command   : $cmd
  82: 
  83: Other sessions are working in this same folder. Switching here moves them too, mid-turn, with no
  84: warning - and if any of them has uncommitted work, that is where it is lost.
  85: 
  86: Do this instead:
  87:   git worktree add <path> <branch>     # a new directory, its own branch, nobody disturbed
  88:   git branch <name> origin/main        # create a branch WITHOUT checking it out
  89: 
  90: An item is worked in its own worktree by its own session. The coordinator stays on main.
  91: "@
  92:     [Console]::Error.Write($msg)
  93:     exit 2
  94: } catch {
  95:     exit 0   # fail OPEN, always
  96: }
